        safety: ['Explosion venting panels or suppression systems designed to applicable standards such as NFPA 68/69 must be installed on the hammer mill and connected ductwork when processing any combustible dust, as the high-speed impact action can generate sparks and the enclosed chamber can accumulate explosive dust-air concentrations that can detonate with devastating force.','Fire detection and suppression systems including spark detectors, thermal sensors, and automatic extinguishing capability must be installed and regularly tested, as the friction between high-speed hammers and material can create hot spots and sparks that ignite accumulated dust or the product itself.','The hammer mill must never be opened for inspection, hammer replacement, or screen changes without first completing full lockout/tagout (LOTO) procedures on all energy sources, as the high-speed rotor carries enormous kinetic energy and can coast for an extended period after power removal, causing fatal injuries upon contact.','Proper electrical grounding and bonding of the hammer mill, ductwork, dust collector, and all connected equipment is essential to prevent static electricity buildup, as electrostatic discharge can serve as an ignition source for combustible dust clouds and cause a devastating explosion.','Dust collection systems must be fully operational and verified effective before starting the hammer mill, as the high-speed grinding action generates large quantities of fine airborne particulate that poses immediate respiratory hazards and creates accumulations that represent both fire and explosion risks.']
