        troubles: [{p:'Gasket failure',c:'Age, chemical attack, overtightening',a:'Replace gaskets, verify compatibility'},{p:'Low efficiency',c:'Fouling, maldistribution, bypass',a:'Clean plates, check gaskets'},{p:'Plate corrosion',c:'Wrong material, concentration cells',a:'Verify material, improve cleaning'},{p:'High pressure drop',c:'Fouling, too many plates, port size',a:'Clean, reconfigure, check ports'}],
        observations: ['Check for external leaks','Monitor approach temperature','Track pressure drop trends','Verify bolt tightening','Inspect gasket condition'],
        safety: ['Gasket failure in a plate heat exchanger can result in external leakage of process fluid to the surroundings or internal cross-contamination between the two fluid streams, and regular gasket inspection and preventive replacement is essential, as a sudden gasket blowout can spray hot or hazardous fluid over a wide area causing burn and chemical exposure injuries.','The plate heat exchanger frame, plates, and piping may be at elevated temperatures during operation, and appropriate warning signs and insulation or guards must be provided to prevent burn injuries, as the compact design means that hot surfaces are often in close proximity to personnel access areas and walkways.','Individual heat exchanger plates are thin, heavy, and have sharp edges, and proper mechanical handling equipment and cut-resistant gloves must be used during plate pack disassembly and reassembly, as manual handling of plates creates risks of hand lacerations, dropped plates causing foot injuries, and musculoskeletal strain from the repetitive heavy lifting.','Chemical cleaning of plate heat exchangers must follow strict procedures including selection of compatible cleaning chemicals, use of appropriate PPE including face shields and chemical suits, proper ventilation, neutralization of spent chemicals, and compliant waste disposal, as the cleaning agents are typically corrosive substances that can cause severe burns and toxic fume exposure.','A hydrostatic pressure test must be performed after every plate heat exchanger reassembly to verify gasket integrity before returning the unit to service, as an improperly assembled plate pack or damaged gasket that is not detected by testing can fail catastrophically under operating conditions, releasing process fluid and causing injuries.']
