        troubles: [{p:'Hot process outlet',c:'Low air flow, fouled fins, fan failure',a:'Check fans, clean fins, verify fan operation'},{p:'High vibration',c:'Fan imbalance, bearing wear, loose blades',a:'Balance fan, replace bearings, tighten'},{p:'Belt failure',c:'Wear, tension, misalignment, heat',a:'Replace belts, adjust tension, align'},{p:'Louver stuck',c:'Corrosion, ice, actuator failure',a:'Free louvers, heat trace, fix actuator'}],
        observations: ['Check fan operation and amperage','Monitor fin cleanliness','Listen for bearing noise','Verify louver operation','Track outlet temperatures'],
        safety: ['The large-diameter fans on air-cooled heat exchangers represent a severe rotating equipment hazard, and all fan openings must be fitted with guards to prevent contact with the spinning blades, as the fans can cause fatal laceration and amputation injuries and can continue to rotate for extended periods after power is removed due to wind effects.','The tube bundles and header boxes contain hot process fluid and their surfaces can reach temperatures exceeding 200 degrees C depending on the service, and appropriate warning signs, access restrictions, and heat-resistant PPE must be provided to prevent severe burn injuries to personnel performing inspections or cleaning fins.','Air-cooled heat exchangers are typically installed at elevated levels on pipe racks or dedicated structures, and all work on fans, tube bundles, louvers, and belts requires proper fall protection including safety harnesses, guardrails, and secured platforms, as falls from these heights are often fatal.','Complete lockout/tagout procedures must be performed on all fan motors and the fans must be confirmed stopped and secured against wind-driven rotation before any person accesses the tube bundle, plenum, or fan area, as unexpected fan startup or wind-driven rotation can cause fatal blade contact injuries.','Personnel working on outdoor air-cooled heat exchangers are exposed to weather hazards including lightning, high winds, rain, ice, and extreme temperatures, and work must be suspended during adverse weather conditions, as wet surfaces create slip hazards, wind increases fall risk, and lightning poses a direct electrocution threat at elevated work positions.']
