        observations: ['Monitor temperature profiles','Track pressure drops per stream','Check cold box vacuum','Watch for ice formation','Verify stream flow balances'],
        safety: ['Plate-fin heat exchangers in cryogenic service operate at extremely low temperatures that can cause instant cryogenic burns and embrittlement of standard carbon steel tools and equipment; appropriate cryogenic PPE including insulated gloves, face shields, and protective clothing must be worn, and only materials rated for cryogenic temperatures may be used in the vicinity.','The cold box enclosure surrounding the plate-fin exchanger can accumulate high concentrations of nitrogen or other inert gases from leaks or purging operations, creating an immediately dangerous oxygen-deficient atmosphere; entry into or around the cold box requires continuous oxygen monitoring and forced ventilation, as asphyxiation can occur without warning.','When handling liquid oxygen (LOX) in plate-fin exchangers, the aluminum construction creates a unique fire hazard, as aluminum can burn vigorously in enriched oxygen atmospheres; all equipment must be rigorously cleaned for oxygen service, and hydrocarbon contamination must be prevented, as even small amounts of organic material in contact with LOX can cause violent combustion.','Plate-fin heat exchangers may operate at high pressures, and the brazed aluminum construction requires careful handling to avoid impact damage that could compromise joints; proper pressure testing, inspection, and overpressure protection must be maintained, as a brazed joint failure can release high-pressure cryogenic fluid with devastating consequences.','Mercury contamination in natural gas feed streams poses a severe risk to brazed aluminum plate-fin exchangers, as mercury causes rapid embrittlement and catastrophic failure of aluminum alloys; mercury removal equipment must be properly maintained upstream, and personnel must follow strict mercury handling procedures due to the severe neurotoxicity of mercury exposure.']
