        safety: ['Graphite heat exchangers are specifically designed for highly corrosive fluids such as strong acids and chloride solutions, and all personnel must wear full chemical-resistant PPE including face shields, acid-resistant suits, and chemical gloves when working on these units, as any leak or spill releases concentrated corrosive fluids that can cause severe chemical burns and tissue destruction.','Graphite blocks are inherently brittle and can crack from impact, thermal shock, or overtightening of bolts, and all handling must use proper lifting equipment with padded contact points and controlled movements, as a cracked block will leak corrosive fluid and a dropped block can shatter, creating sharp fragments and releasing hazardous contents.','The surfaces of graphite heat exchangers may be at elevated temperatures during operation depending on the service, and appropriate warning signs and heat-resistant, chemical-resistant PPE must be provided, as the combination of hot surfaces and corrosive fluid leaks creates a dual hazard of thermal burns compounded by chemical burns.','Chemical cleaning of graphite heat exchangers must be performed with careful consideration of chemical compatibility, as the impregnating resin and gasket materials may be attacked by certain cleaning agents; all cleaning operations require appropriate PPE, ventilation, and waste handling procedures, as the residual process fluids combined with cleaning chemicals can produce toxic fumes.','Full personal protective equipment rated for acid service must be worn by all personnel working on graphite heat exchangers, including acid-resistant face shields, chemical splash goggles, acid-resistant gloves and suits, and appropriate respiratory protection, as the corrosive fluids handled by these units can cause irreversible tissue damage upon skin or eye contact and produce harmful vapors.']
