        observations: ['Monitor tube skin temperatures','Check flame pattern at burners','Track stack temperature and O2','Inspect refractory condition','Verify draft balance'],
        safety: ['The fired heater presents a constant fire and explosion hazard due to the presence of open flames, hot surfaces, combustible fuel, and process hydrocarbons; strict hot work procedures, combustible gas monitoring, and functional burner management safety systems must be maintained at all times to prevent catastrophic fires and explosions.','Virtually all surfaces of the cabin heater including the casing, flue gas ductwork, process piping, tube returns, and stack are at elevated temperatures that can cause instant severe burns upon contact, and comprehensive insulation, warning signs, and heat-resistant PPE must be provided for all personnel working near the heater.','A process tube rupture inside the firebox will release flammable hydrocarbons directly into the combustion zone, creating an immediate fire and potential explosion; emergency shutdown systems, flame detection, and relief devices must be maintained in operational condition, and personnel must know the location of emergency exits and rally points.','Entry into the firebox for inspection, refractory repair, or tube work constitutes a confined space entry in an environment with residual combustion products, and requires a formal permit, atmospheric testing for oxygen, CO, H2S, and hydrocarbons, forced ventilation, a standby attendant, and rescue equipment.','Personnel working near fired heaters are at risk of exposure to carbon monoxide (CO) from incomplete combustion and hydrogen sulfide (H2S) from sulfur-containing fuels or process fluids, both of which are toxic at low concentrations; continuous gas monitoring with audible alarms must be maintained, as CO is odorless and H2S causes olfactory fatigue.']
