        observations: ['Infrared scanning of tubes','Monitor tube metal temps','Track conversion and exit temps','Check burner flame patterns','Inspect tube hangers'],
        safety: ['Reformer furnaces operate at very high temperatures with tube wall temperatures of 800-950 degrees C, and all surfaces including the furnace shell, piping, manifolds, and transfer lines are at extreme temperatures; comprehensive insulation and strict exclusion zones must be maintained, as contact with any surface will cause instant severe burns.','The reformer operates in a hydrogen-rich atmosphere with hydrogen and carbon monoxide present in the process gas, and all piping, flanges, and fittings must be leak-free, as hydrogen is odorless, invisible when burning, and explosive in a wide concentration range, creating extreme fire and explosion hazards that are difficult to detect visually.','A catalyst tube rupture releases synthesis gas containing hydrogen and carbon monoxide directly into the firebox, creating an immediate explosion and toxic gas hazard; emergency shutdown systems must be functional and regularly tested, and personnel must be trained in emergency response procedures, as syngas release incidents can rapidly become fatal.','The refractory lining inside the reformer furnace operates at extreme temperatures and retains heat for extended periods after shutdown, and personnel must wait for adequate cooldown and verify surface temperatures before entry or contact, as the hot refractory surfaces will cause instant severe burns and the enclosed firebox traps radiant heat.','Spent nickel-based reforming catalyst may contain hazardous substances including nickel compounds that are classified as carcinogenic, and proper PPE including respiratory protection, chemical gloves, and protective clothing must be worn during catalyst handling, loading, and unloading operations, with all waste catalyst disposed of according to applicable hazardous waste regulations.']
