        observations: ['Monitor coil outlet temps','Track run length between decokes','Check TLE outlet temps','Verify dilution steam ratio','Inspect tube condition'],
        safety: ['Pyrolysis furnaces operate at extreme temperatures with coil outlet temperatures of 800-875 degrees C, and all personnel must maintain safe distances from the radiant section and transfer lines, as these surfaces are at temperatures that will cause fatal burns upon even momentary contact.','The entire pyrolysis furnace operates in a hydrocarbon-rich atmosphere with cracked gases including ethylene, propylene, and hydrogen present throughout the process, and continuous gas detection, proper ventilation, and strict ignition source control must be maintained, as any hydrocarbon leak in combination with the high temperatures creates an immediate explosion and fire hazard.','Decoking procedures involving the injection of steam and air into the hot cracking coils at temperatures exceeding 800 degrees C are inherently hazardous and must follow strict written procedures, as improper decoking can cause tube overheating, runaway combustion of coke deposits, tube rupture, and release of hot gases and flames.','Hot surfaces are present throughout the entire pyrolysis furnace system including the radiant section casing, convection section, transfer lines, transfer line exchangers, and associated piping, and comprehensive insulation and warning signage must be maintained, as even brief contact with any uninsulated surface will cause severe to fatal burn injuries.','The pyrolysis furnace system includes high-pressure steam generation in the convection section and transfer line exchangers operating at pressures exceeding 100 bar, and all steam system components must be properly rated, inspected, and maintained, as a high-pressure steam failure can release superheated steam with explosive force causing fatal burn and blast injuries.']
