        observations: ['Monitor circulation flow','Check tube skin temperatures','Track radiant section draft','Verify two-phase separation','Inspect tube supports'],
        safety: ['Vertical tube heaters in two-phase service are subject to surging caused by unstable boiling and vapor slug formation, which can cause violent pressure pulsations, hammering in connected piping, and sudden liquid discharge from vents or relief devices; proper design and operating procedures must address these dynamic hazards to prevent piping failures and personnel injuries.','The vertical tubes and associated piping at elevated levels operate at high temperatures, and working at height near hot surfaces combines fall hazards with burn hazards; proper fall protection and heat-resistant PPE must be used simultaneously, and access platforms must be designed to maintain safe distances from hot tube surfaces.','Natural circulation in vertical tube heaters can become unstable due to changes in heat input, liquid level, or downstream pressure, leading to sudden flow reversal, vapor lock, or loss of circulation that can cause rapid tube overheating and failure; operators must monitor circulation indicators closely and respond immediately to any signs of circulation upset.','A tube rupture in the fired section releases process fluid directly into the firebox, creating an immediate fire and potential explosion; emergency shutdown systems must be tested and functional, relief devices properly sized, and all personnel must be trained in emergency response, as tube failures in fired heaters can escalate to catastrophic incidents within seconds.','The combination of open flame from burners, high-temperature surfaces, and process hydrocarbons creates persistent fire hazards requiring continuous monitoring, functional fire detection and suppression systems, and strict hot work controls; all personnel must know the location of firefighting equipment and emergency escape routes.']
