        observations: ['Track each effect temperature','Monitor vacuum levels','Check steam consumption','Verify balance between effects','Watch condensate quality'],
        safety: ['Multiple effect evaporator systems consist of several pressure vessels operating at different pressures and temperatures, each of which must be individually rated, inspected, and equipped with functional relief devices, as failure of any single vessel can cause cascading failures in the interconnected system with catastrophic release of hot fluid and steam.','The later effects in a multiple effect evaporator system operate under vacuum, and all vacuum-rated vessels and piping must be designed to withstand full vacuum conditions, as sudden loss of vacuum can cause implosion of under-rated components, and opening connections on vacuum systems can cause rapid inrush of air and fluid creating impact and splash hazards.','Hot surfaces are present throughout the entire multiple effect evaporator system, with the first effect at the highest temperature and subsequent effects at progressively lower but still hazardous temperatures, and comprehensive insulation and warning signs must be provided across all effects to prevent burn injuries.','The interconnected nature of multiple effect evaporators makes isolation for maintenance complex, as each effect is connected to adjacent effects by vapor lines, condensate lines, and product transfer piping, and proper isolation procedures must address every connection to ensure complete depressurization and decontamination before any vessel is opened for maintenance.','Multiple effect evaporator systems are typically arranged in multi-level structures requiring frequent work at height for valve operation, instrumentation access, and maintenance, and proper fall protection including guardrails, safety harnesses, and secured platforms must be provided at all elevated work locations, as falls from these industrial structures are often fatal.']
