        observations: ['Check flash chamber levels','Monitor vacuum levels','Track distillate production','Verify temperature drops','Watch for scaling'],
        safety: ['Flash evaporator stages operating under vacuum must be designed and rated for full vacuum conditions with proper vacuum relief devices, as sudden loss of vacuum can cause implosion of the flash chambers, and the multiple interconnected stages require careful attention to the pressure rating of each vessel and connecting piping.','The hot brine circulating through flash evaporator stages is at or near boiling temperature and is concentrated with dissolved salts, creating a dual hazard of thermal burns and chemical irritation; appropriate heat-resistant and chemical-resistant PPE must be worn, and containment must be provided for any potential brine release.','Flash evaporators operate with multiple stages at different pressure levels ranging from above atmospheric in the first stages to deep vacuum in the last stages, and personnel must be aware that each stage presents different pressure hazards; proper isolation and depressurization procedures must be followed for the specific stage being maintained.','Chemical treatment agents including scale inhibitors, antifoams, and corrosion inhibitors are used throughout the flash evaporator system, and proper handling procedures including chemical-resistant PPE, spill containment, and material safety data sheet review must be followed, as these treatment chemicals can cause skin burns, eye damage, and respiratory irritation.','All electrical systems in the flash evaporator facility including motors, instruments, lighting, and controls operate in a wet, potentially corrosive environment and must be rated accordingly with proper waterproof enclosures, ground fault protection, and regular insulation testing, as the combination of electricity and wet conditions creates a serious risk of electrocution.']
