        troubles: [{p:'Burner fouling',c:'Deposits, poor combustion',a:'Clean burner, adjust combustion'},{p:'Corrosion',c:'Acidic conditions, chlorides',a:'Material selection, pH control'},{p:'Emissions',c:'Incomplete combustion, carryover',a:'Tune burner, improve scrubbing'},{p:'Foaming',c:'Organics, surfactants',a:'Add antifoam, reduce rate'}],
        observations: ['Monitor combustion quality','Check exhaust composition','Track evaporation rate','Verify liquid level','Watch for foaming'],
        safety: ['The submerged combustion evaporator operates with an open flame and hot combustion gases bubbling directly through the process liquid, creating a unique and inherently hazardous configuration; strict burner management procedures, flame monitoring, and safe operating limits must be maintained, as loss of flame supervision can allow unburned fuel to accumulate in the liquid with explosive consequences.','Combustible fuel gas is supplied to the submerged burner at pressure, and all gas supply piping, valves, regulators, and safety shutoff devices must be properly maintained and leak-tested regularly, as a gas leak in the vicinity of the hot evaporator creates an immediate fire and explosion hazard that can cause fatal injuries and facility destruction.','The exhaust gases from submerged combustion exit at high temperature and may contain entrained liquid droplets, and the exhaust system including scrubbers and stacks must be properly designed and maintained, as hot exhaust can cause severe burns and may contain incompletely combusted fuel or process chemicals that are hazardous to inhale.','The concentrated liquid in the evaporator tank may be highly corrosive depending on the application, and all personnel must wear appropriate chemical-resistant PPE including face shields, chemical suits, and acid-resistant gloves when working near the tank or handling the concentrated product, as splashing from the bubbling action can project corrosive liquid onto personnel.','The submerged burner presents a risk of explosion if fuel is introduced without proper ignition, if the flame is extinguished and fuel continues to flow, or if combustible vapors accumulate above the liquid surface; a properly designed and maintained burner management system with automatic fuel shutoff is essential to prevent catastrophic explosions.']
