        safety: ['Spray dryers present a significant dust explosion hazard as the fine powder product dispersed in hot air within the drying chamber can form explosive dust-air mixtures, and comprehensive explosion protection including proper grounding, explosion venting or suppression, powder concentration monitoring, and elimination of ignition sources must be implemented to prevent catastrophic explosions.','Multiple hot surfaces throughout the spray dryer system including the drying chamber walls, air heater, inlet ductwork, and exhaust piping operate at elevated temperatures up to 300 degrees C, and appropriate insulation, warning signs, and heat-resistant PPE must be provided to prevent severe burn injuries to personnel.','The spray dryer drying chamber is a very large vessel requiring work at height for atomizer maintenance, inspection of the chamber interior, and access to the air disperser, and proper fall protection including safety harnesses, guardrails, and secured work platforms must be provided, as falls from the top of these tall vessels are fatal.','Entry into the spray dryer chamber for inspection, cleaning, or wall deposit removal constitutes confined space entry requiring a formal permit, atmospheric testing for oxygen and combustible dust, forced ventilation, a standby attendant, and rescue equipment, as the large enclosed chamber can contain oxygen-depleted atmospheres and combustible dust accumulations.','Fire detection and suppression systems must be installed in the spray dryer chamber, cyclone, bag filter, and ductwork, as product deposits on hot surfaces can char and ignite, and smoldering material can propagate through the system; automatic suppression using water, steam, or inert gas must be capable of rapid response to prevent fire escalation.']
