        observations: ['Monitor homogenizing pressure','Check product particle size','Verify pump operation','Watch for leaks','Track valve life'],
        safety: ['Homogenizers operate at extreme pressures up to 400 MPa, and any failure of high-pressure components including plunger seals, valve seats, or piping connections can result in high-velocity fluid jets capable of causing injection injuries, severe lacerations, or fatal penetrating wounds to nearby personnel.','The homogenizing valve and impact ring are subject to extreme wear and erosion from high-velocity fluid flow, and catastrophic valve failure can release high-pressure fluid instantaneously, requiring regular inspection, replacement at specified intervals, and installation of protective shields around the valve assembly.','The high-pressure homogenization process generates intense noise levels typically exceeding 95 dB, and all personnel working in the vicinity must wear appropriate hearing protection such as double protection (earplugs plus earmuffs) to prevent permanent noise-induced hearing damage from prolonged exposure.','Mechanical energy conversion during homogenization generates significant heat, causing the homogenizer head, discharge piping, and product to reach elevated temperatures that can cause burns on contact, requiring thermal insulation on accessible surfaces and awareness that product temperature increases with operating pressure.','High-pressure components under extreme stress can fail catastrophically, sending valve fragments, seal components, or pipe sections flying at high velocity, requiring protective guarding around the homogenizing head and prohibition of personnel from standing in line with high-pressure connections during operation.']
