        safety: ['The venturi throat operates at gas velocities of 60-120 m/s, creating extreme turbulence and pressure conditions, and any structural failure, gasket blow-out, or access opening at the throat will release a high-energy jet of gas and liquid that can cause severe impact and inhalation injuries.','Venturi scrubbers generate extremely high noise levels (often exceeding 100 dB) from the high-velocity gas flow through the throat section, and all personnel working in the area must wear appropriate hearing protection such as earplugs with earmuffs to prevent immediate and permanent hearing damage.','Liquid spray, condensation, and overflow from the scrubber and separator create extensively wet and slippery conditions in the surrounding area, presenting serious slip and fall hazards that require non-slip flooring, proper drainage, and slip-resistant footwear to prevent injuries.','The atomized scrubbing liquid mixed with captured particles creates a fine chemical mist that can remain airborne and be inhaled by personnel, potentially containing dissolved toxic gases, suspended particulates, and acid droplets that cause respiratory damage, requiring effective mist elimination and respiratory protection for nearby workers.','The high gas velocities carrying abrasive dust particles cause rapid erosion of the throat section and downstream components, and erosion-weakened walls can fail suddenly, releasing gas and contaminated liquid, requiring regular wall thickness measurements and replacement of worn components before they reach minimum safe thickness.']
