        safety: ['Ceramic filters operate at extreme temperatures up to 900°C, and all external surfaces, housings, and associated ductwork can cause instantaneous severe burns upon contact, requiring comprehensive insulation, thermal barriers, warning signs, and heat-resistant PPE for any work in the vicinity.','Even after shutdown, ceramic filter housings and internal components retain extreme heat for extended periods, and personnel must verify that temperatures have decreased to safe levels before any approach or entry, as thermal inertia in the refractory lining can maintain burn-causing temperatures for hours after process shutdown.','Ceramic filter candles and tubes are brittle and fragile, and careless handling can cause breakage that wastes expensive elements and creates sharp fragment hazards, requiring gentle handling techniques, specialized lifting tools, and cut-resistant gloves during all element installation, removal, and inspection activities.','The ceramic filter housing is a confined space that may contain residual hazardous dust, oxygen-depleted atmospheres, and extremely hot surfaces, requiring full confined space entry procedures including extended cool-down verification, atmospheric testing, ventilation, and standby rescue before any person enters the vessel.','Thermal expansion of the filter housing, tube sheet, and ceramic elements during heat-up and cool-down creates significant mechanical stresses that can crack elements and damage seals, and personnel must maintain safe distances during thermal transitions when sudden element failure could release hot gas and dust through the housing.']
