        observations: ['Monitor vibration amplitude','Check spring condition','Verify material flow','Listen for changes','Watch drive temperature'],
        safety: ['Personnel who regularly work near or perform maintenance on vibrating conveyors are at risk of hand-arm vibration syndrome (HAVS) and whole-body vibration injuries, so direct contact with vibrating surfaces should be minimized and anti-vibration gloves should be worn when physical contact is necessary.','The springs, trough mounts, and drive connections on vibrating conveyors create multiple pinch and crush points that can trap fingers or hands, so all guards must remain in place during operation and lockout/tagout must be performed before any maintenance or adjustment work.','When vibrating conveyors are used for hot material such as freshly dried or calcined products, the trough surface temperature can cause severe contact burns, so hot surface warning labels must be displayed and thermal-resistant gloves must be worn when working near the equipment.','Vibrating conveyors generate sustained noise levels that commonly exceed 85 dB during operation, so all personnel working within the noise zone must wear appropriate hearing protection such as earplugs or earmuffs to prevent cumulative hearing loss over time.','The trough of a vibrating conveyor moves rapidly and with considerable force during operation, creating a struck-by hazard for anyone standing too close or attempting to reach into the moving trough, so personnel must maintain safe distances and never place hands or tools on the trough while it is running.']
