        observations: ['Monitor pressure','Check level','Verify relief valve','Watch for leaks','Track inspection dates'],
        safety: ['Pressure storage vessels contain significant stored energy that can cause catastrophic failure with lethal consequences if the vessel is overpressured, corroded, or mechanically damaged, so all pressure relief devices must be maintained, inspection schedules must be followed, and operating pressure must never exceed the nameplate design rating.','Releasing pressure from storage vessels must be done through properly designed and piped vent or blowdown systems, as uncontrolled pressure release can propel valve parts, fittings, or vessel fragments at lethal velocity, and the released contents may be flammable, toxic, or at extreme temperatures.','Entry into pressure vessels for inspection or repair is a confined space operation requiring a permit, atmospheric testing, isolation of all inlets and outlets by blinding or double block and bleed, continuous ventilation, and a trained standby attendant with rescue equipment.','Vessels storing liquefied petroleum gas (LPG), propane, or other cryogenic liquids can cause severe cold burns and frostbite upon contact with the released liquid, so personnel must wear cryogenic-rated gloves, face shields, and protective clothing when working on connections, valves, or relief devices.','The contents of pressure storage vessels may be toxic, corrosive, or asphyxiating, so personnel must review the safety data sheets for all stored materials, wear appropriate personal protective equipment, ensure gas detection is available, and have emergency response procedures in place before performing any work on the vessel.']
