        safety: ['Walking on a floating roof tank presents fall and engulfment hazards because the roof surface may be uneven, slippery from product or rainwater, or structurally weakened by corrosion, so personnel must use designated walkways, wear fall protection, and verify roof structural integrity before stepping onto any floating roof.','Flammable vapors can accumulate around the rim seal area, in pontoon compartments, and on the roof surface of floating roof tanks, so atmospheric testing must be performed before any work, hot work permits must be obtained, and all ignition sources must be eliminated in the immediate vicinity.','The space beneath a floating roof and above the stored liquid is a confined space with potentially lethal concentrations of hydrocarbon vapors and oxygen-deficient air, so no person should enter this space without full confined space entry procedures, supplied air breathing apparatus, and continuous atmospheric monitoring.','All equipment, hoses, and tools used on or near floating roof tanks must be properly bonded to the tank shell and grounded to prevent static electricity accumulation, as a static discharge near the rim seal or roof openings can ignite flammable vapors and cause a catastrophic tank fire.','A comprehensive rescue plan with trained rescue personnel and appropriate equipment must be established before any work on floating roof tanks, as the height, confined spaces, and potential for vapor exposure create multiple scenarios where rapid emergency extraction may be needed to save lives.']
