        components: ['Inner Vessel - cryogenic containment','Outer Jacket - insulation space','Insulation - vacuum or perlite','Relief Valves - pressure protection','Level Gauge - cryogenic service','Vaporizer - liquid to gas','Vacuum System - if vacuum type','Support - inner vessel mount'],
        troubles: [{p:'High boil-off',c:'Vacuum loss, insulation damage',a:'Check vacuum, repair insulation'},{p:'Ice formation',c:'Moisture ingress',a:'Purge, dry, repair'},{p:'Level gauge failure',c:'Freeze-up, damage',a:'Thaw, repair, replace'},{p:'Relief discharge',c:'High pressure buildup',a:'Use product, check control'}],
        observations: ['Monitor pressure','Check vacuum level','Verify level reading','Watch for ice/frost','Track boil-off rate'],
        safety: ['Cryogenic liquids at temperatures as low as minus 196 degrees Celsius will cause severe cold burns, frostbite, and tissue destruction on contact with skin, so personnel must wear cryogenic-rated gloves, face shields, splash-proof aprons, and closed-toe shoes when working near cryogenic connections, valves, or potential leak points.','Cryogenic liquids expand enormously when vaporized, and the resulting gas can rapidly displace oxygen in enclosed or poorly ventilated areas, creating an invisible asphyxiation hazard that can cause unconsciousness and death within minutes, so oxygen monitors must be worn and adequate ventilation must be ensured in all areas where cryogenic leaks could occur.','Cryogenic storage tanks operate under pressure from natural boil-off of the stored liquid, and overpressure can result in catastrophic vessel failure, so all pressure relief devices must be maintained and tested, pressure gauges must be monitored, and the tank must never be isolated from its relief system.','Exposure of carbon steel piping, structural supports, and other non-cryogenic-rated materials to cryogenic temperatures causes brittle fracture and catastrophic failure, so any leak or spill of cryogenic liquid must be promptly addressed and all materials in contact with cryogenic service must be verified as suitable for the operating temperature.','All personnel working with or near cryogenic systems must wear complete personal protective equipment including cryogenic gloves, face shield, safety glasses, long-sleeved clothing, and closed-toe leather shoes, as the extreme cold can cause rapid and severe injury to any exposed skin or tissue.']
