        description: 'Components connecting, branching, and changing direction of piping. Elbows, tees, reducers, and couplings. Threaded, socket weld, or butt weld types.',
        principles: 'Shape changes flow direction or pipe size. Pressure rating matches pipe schedule. Welded for permanent, threaded for small sizes. Material matches pipe.',
        specs: [{t:'Types',v:'Elbow, tee, reducer'},{t:'Connection',v:'Welded, threaded, flanged'},{t:'Material',v:'Match pipe'},{t:'Rating',v:'Match system pressure'}],
        components: ['Fitting Body - shaped component','End Connections - pipe attachment','Wall Thickness - pressure rating','Bevel - for welding','Threads - if threaded','Socket - if socket weld','Branch - if tee or wye','Radius - if elbow'],
        troubles: [{p:'Leakage',c:'Poor weld, thread damage',a:'Repair weld, remake thread'},{p:'Erosion',c:'High velocity, solids',a:'Thicker wall, lower velocity'},{p:'Corrosion',c:'Wrong material',a:'Replace with proper material'},{p:'Cracking',c:'Stress, fatigue',a:'Support, stress relief'}],
        observations: ['Inspect for leaks','Check for erosion','Monitor wall thickness','Verify support','Watch for corrosion'],
        safety: ['All pipe fittings must be fabricated from materials compatible with the process fluid and rated for the system pressure and temperature, as the use of incorrect materials can result in corrosion, cracking, or mechanical failure leading to sudden release of hazardous process fluid and potential injury or environmental contamination.','Pipe fittings must be rated for the maximum operating pressure and temperature of the system, including any upset or emergency conditions, as a fitting that fails under pressure can release process fluid at high velocity causing burns, chemical exposure, or impact injuries, and the weakest fitting in a system determines the overall system rating.','Pipe fittings, especially at branch connections, reducers, and direction changes, must be accessible for inspection and non-destructive examination, as these locations are prone to erosion, corrosion, and stress cracking that may not be visible externally, and undetected wall thinning can lead to sudden blowout during normal operation.','Pipe fittings at changes of direction, branching, and dead ends must be properly supported to prevent excessive stress from weight, thermal expansion, and dynamic loads such as water hammer, as unsupported fittings can develop fatigue cracks or separate from the pipe, causing loss of containment.','Piping systems experience significant dimensional changes due to thermal expansion and contraction, and fittings at fixed points, anchors, and equipment connections must be designed to accommodate these movements, as excessive thermal stress on fittings can cause cracking, weld failure, and loss of pressure containment.']
