        observations: ['Compare to other readings','Check response time','Verify pointer movement','Watch for damage','Monitor thermowell'],
        safety: ['Temperature gauge stems, thermowells, and adjacent piping in hot service can reach surface temperatures that cause severe burns on contact, so personnel must wear thermal protective gloves when handling temperature gauges and must be aware that the gauge stem and thermowell are at process temperature even when the dial face appears cool.','Thermowell failure due to vibration-induced fatigue, corrosion, or excessive flow velocity can result in a process breach at the thermowell nozzle, releasing pressurized and potentially hazardous process fluid into the environment, so thermowell integrity must be verified through wake frequency calculations and regular inspection.','Removing a temperature gauge or thermowell from a steam or hot pressurized system without proper isolation will result in release of live steam or hot fluid through the open nozzle, causing severe burn injuries, so the process must be isolated and depressurized before any gauge or thermowell removal, and appropriate PPE must be worn.','Temperature gauges must be installed at the correct insertion depth and angle to ensure accurate measurement, and the thermowell must be properly torqued and sealed to prevent process leaks, as an improperly installed thermowell can loosen during thermal cycling, creating a leak path for hazardous process fluid.','Withdrawing a thermowell from a pressurized process connection creates a temporary opening through which process fluid can escape, so the system must be depressurized and isolated before thermowell removal, and the nozzle must be plugged or capped immediately after thermowell extraction to prevent release of residual process fluid.']
