        components: ['Main Headers - distribution','Pressure Reducing Stations - level control','Steam Traps - condensate removal','Insulation - heat conservation','Expansion Loops - thermal growth','Isolation Valves - sectioning','Drains - low point drainage','Condensate Return - water recovery'],
        troubles: [{p:'Water hammer',c:'Condensate accumulation',a:'Improve trapping and drainage'},{p:'Pressure variation',c:'Load changes, PRV',a:'Check PRV, balance loads'},{p:'Heat loss',c:'Poor insulation',a:'Repair insulation'},{p:'Trap failures',c:'Blow-through, stuck',a:'Test and replace traps'}],
        observations: ['Monitor pressures','Check trap operation','Verify insulation','Listen for water hammer','Track condensate return'],
        safety: ['Steam at any pressure can cause severe, potentially fatal burns on contact with skin, as steam carries enormous thermal energy and will condense on cooler surfaces releasing its latent heat, so all personnel must maintain safe distances from steam leaks, wear appropriate thermal protective equipment, and immediately evacuate the area if a steam leak is detected.','Water hammer in steam distribution systems occurs when accumulated condensate is picked up and accelerated by steam flow, then slammed against pipe fittings, valves, or direction changes with destructive force that can rupture piping and fittings, so proper condensate drainage, steam trap maintenance, and gradual warm-up procedures must be followed to prevent water hammer events.','Steam distribution systems operate at pressures that can cause catastrophic piping failure with violent release of steam and hot condensate, so all pressure-containing components must be maintained, inspected per applicable codes, and operated within their design pressure and temperature limits, and pressure relief devices must be functional at all times.','All steam pipes, valves, fittings, and equipment surfaces operate at temperatures sufficient to cause severe burns on contact, so comprehensive insulation and lagging must be maintained on all accessible steam system components, hot surface warning signs must be posted, and personnel must wear thermal protective gloves when working on any steam system component.','Condensate trapped in steam piping, drip legs, or improperly drained equipment can be at steam temperature and pressure, and opening a drain or vent on a section containing trapped condensate can result in sudden release of hot water and flash steam, so all drains must be opened slowly, personnel must stand clear of the discharge path, and appropriate PPE must be worn.']
