        troubles: [{p:'Transformer trip',c:'Overload, fault',a:'Check protection, investigate'},{p:'Breaker trip',c:'Overload, fault',a:'Find fault, reset carefully'},{p:'Power quality',c:'Harmonics, sag',a:'Add filters, check system'},{p:'Grounding issues',c:'Connection, soil',a:'Verify continuity, test ground'}],
        observations: ['Monitor transformer loading','Check breaker status','Verify voltage levels','Watch for alarms','Track power factor'],
        safety: ['Electrical substations contain equipment capable of producing arc flash events with temperatures exceeding 35,000 degrees Fahrenheit and blast pressures that can throw a person across the room, causing severe burns, blast injuries, and death, so arc flash hazard analysis must be performed, appropriate arc-rated PPE must be worn, and safe approach boundaries must be maintained per NFPA 70E.','Electrical substations contain exposed conductors and equipment operating at voltages from hundreds to thousands of volts that can cause immediate electrocution and death upon contact, so all substation areas must be secured with locked enclosures, only authorized personnel should enter, and no conductive objects should be brought near energized equipment.','High voltage equipment in substations operates at potential levels that can arc across air gaps and through insulation, making proximity alone potentially lethal without direct contact, so minimum approach distances must be strictly observed, and all work near energized high voltage equipment must be performed only by qualified electrical workers using proper procedures and tools rated for the voltage level.','Only qualified electrical personnel who have been trained and certified in high voltage safety procedures, arc flash protection, and the specific equipment installed should be permitted to enter, operate, or perform work on electrical substation equipment, as untrained personnel cannot recognize or protect themselves from the life-threatening hazards present.','All electrical equipment in substations must be locked out and tagged out with verified zero energy state before any maintenance, repair, or modification work begins, following OSHA lockout/tagout requirements and NFPA 70E procedures, as unexpected energization of equipment during maintenance is a leading cause of electrical fatalities in industrial settings.']
