        troubles: [{p:'Smoking flare',c:'Insufficient assist',a:'Increase steam or air'},{p:'Flame-out',c:'Pilot failure, wind',a:'Relight, check pilots'},{p:'Liquid carryover',c:'Knockout failure',a:'Check knockout, drain liquids'},{p:'Flashback',c:'Seal failure, low flow',a:'Verify seal, maintain purge'}],
        observations: ['Monitor flame','Check knockout level','Verify pilots lit','Watch for smoke','Track purge flow'],
        safety: ['The flare flame produces intense thermal radiation that can cause severe burns to exposed skin and ignite combustible materials at significant distances from the flare tip, so thermal radiation exclusion zones must be established and enforced, and personnel must not enter high radiation areas without proper thermal protective clothing and for limited duration only.','The flare flame and associated pilot flames present a direct fire hazard to personnel working near the flare tip, on the flare stack structure, or near ground flare enclosures, so no personnel should work near an active flare without a permit, and all maintenance requiring proximity to the flare tip must be performed only when the flare is fully isolated and pilots are extinguished.','Flare systems collecting relief gases from process units may contain hydrogen sulfide (H2S) and other toxic gases that can leak from flanges, valves, or the knockout drum, creating immediately dangerous to life and health conditions, so personal H2S monitors must be worn, wind direction must be monitored, and emergency escape routes must be planned when working near flare headers.','Flare stacks are tall structures that require working at significant heights for inspection, maintenance of the flare tip, and monitoring equipment, creating fall hazards that require proper fall protection equipment, climbing safety systems, and rescue plans, and all elevated work must be performed by personnel trained and certified for working at heights.','Flare systems are critical safety systems designed to safely dispose of emergency and relief gas discharges, and their availability must be maintained at all times when the process units they protect are in operation, so any work that could affect flare system availability must be managed through a strict permit and risk assessment process.']
