        category: 'Utilities',
        description: 'Dedicated water systems for fire suppression. Pumps, distribution, hydrants, and monitors. Reliability critical for emergency response.',
        principles: 'Dedicated pumps maintain pressure. Ring main distributes water. Hydrants and monitors provide access. Sprinklers and deluge systems protect equipment.',
        specs: [{t:'Pressure',v:'Design for coverage'},{t:'Pumps',v:'Electric and diesel backup'},{t:'Storage',v:'Design duration'},{t:'Distribution',v:'Ring main preferred'}],
        components: ['Fire Pumps - pressure and flow','Jockey Pump - pressure maintenance','Storage Tank - water supply','Ring Main - distribution','Hydrants - hose connections','Monitors - fixed nozzles','Deluge Systems - area protection','Sprinklers - automatic'],
        troubles: [{p:'Low pressure',c:'Pump, leak, valve',a:'Check pumps, find leak'},{p:'Pump wont start',c:'Power, controller, engine',a:'Check power, controller, fuel'},{p:'Frozen pipes',c:'No heat trace, drain',a:'Heat trace, insulate, drain'},{p:'Valve stuck',c:'No exercise, corrosion',a:'Exercise regularly, replace'}],
        observations: ['Monitor header pressure','Test pumps weekly','Exercise valves','Check tank level','Verify alarm function'],
        safety: ['Fire water systems are life safety and asset protection systems that must be maintained in a ready state at all times, with all components functional and unobstructed, as any impairment of the fire water system during a fire event can result in uncontrolled fire spread, loss of life, and catastrophic property damage.','Fire hydrants, monitors, hose connections, sprinkler valves, and access routes to fire water equipment must be kept clear and accessible at all times, and no equipment, vehicles, materials, or temporary structures should block access to any fire protection equipment, as seconds of delay in accessing fire water during an emergency can result in loss of life.','Fire water pumps, valves, hydrants, and sprinkler systems must be tested on a regular schedule per NFPA standards to verify their operational readiness, as equipment that is not regularly tested may fail to operate when needed during an actual emergency, and all test results must be documented and deficiencies corrected promptly.','Fire water pumps including electric and diesel-driven backup pumps must be tested weekly and maintained per NFPA 25, and all operators and emergency response personnel must be trained in pump startup procedures, as pump failure during a fire event eliminates the primary means of fire suppression and can lead to total loss of the facility.','Discharge of large volumes of fire water during testing or emergency response can cause water damage to electrical equipment, instruments, and process materials, and contaminated fire water runoff can cause environmental damage, so water management plans must be in place, drains must be adequate, and environmental containment must be considered during fire water operations.']
