        observations: ['Compare to other measurements','Check for air in lines','Verify transmitter output','Watch for coating','Track calibration'],
        safety: ['Impulse lines connecting differential pressure flow meters to the process piping contain pressurized process fluid and can leak at fittings, valves, or due to corrosion, releasing hazardous materials, so impulse lines must be regularly inspected, personnel must wear appropriate PPE when working on impulse tubing, and lines must be depressurized before disconnecting any fitting.','Flow meter transmitters and associated wiring operate on electrical power that presents shock hazards, and some flow meters such as magnetic types operate at higher voltages, so all electrical work must be performed by qualified personnel, power must be locked out before opening junction boxes, and intrinsic safety barriers must be maintained for instruments in hazardous areas.','Working on flow meter installations including primary elements, impulse lines, and transmitters may expose personnel to process fluids that can be toxic, corrosive, hot, or under pressure, so appropriate personal protective equipment must be worn, process isolation must be confirmed, and safety data sheets for the process fluid must be reviewed before beginning work.','Installation or removal of inline flow meters such as orifice plates, magnetic meters, and Coriolis meters in piping located in pits, trenches, or enclosed spaces may constitute confined space work requiring atmospheric testing, ventilation, and a standby attendant, especially if the piping has contained hazardous materials.','Flow meters installed in hot process services such as steam, thermal oil, or high-temperature chemical streams will have surfaces at process temperature that can cause burns on contact, so personnel must wear thermal protective gloves when working on these instruments and must be aware of the temperature of all components in the flow meter installation.']
