        observations: ['Compare to sight glass','Check for coating','Verify calibration','Watch for foam effects','Track density changes'],
        safety: ['Installation, calibration, or maintenance of level instruments mounted on vessels may require accessing the vessel interior, which constitutes confined space entry requiring a written permit, atmospheric testing, isolation of all process connections, and a standby attendant, as vessels may contain residual hazardous vapors or oxygen-deficient atmospheres.','Working on level instrument process connections, bridles, and chambers exposes personnel to process fluids that may be toxic, corrosive, flammable, or at elevated pressure and temperature, so appropriate PPE must be worn, the instrument must be isolated and drained before opening any process connection, and safety data sheets must be reviewed for the process fluid.','Level transmitter electrical connections must be properly sealed against moisture intrusion and must be installed in appropriately rated enclosures for the area classification, and all electrical work must be performed by qualified personnel with proper lockout/tagout, as improper electrical connections in hazardous areas can create ignition sources for flammable atmospheres.','Level instruments installed on vessels containing hot or cryogenic fluids will have external components at extreme temperatures that can cause burns or cold injuries on contact, so thermal protective equipment must be worn when working on these instruments, and personnel must be aware that bridles and chambers contain process fluid at the vessel operating temperature.','Nuclear level gauges using radioactive sources such as Cesium-137 or Cobalt-60 present radiation exposure hazards, and all work on or near nuclear level instruments must be performed only by personnel trained and licensed in radiation safety, following strict protocols for exposure time, distance, and shielding, with radiation dosimeters worn at all times.']
