        observations: ['Compare to gauge','Check impulse lines','Verify calibration','Watch for coating','Monitor diagnostics'],
        safety: ['Pressure transmitters are connected to process systems operating under pressure, and the transmitter housing, impulse lines, and manifold valves all contain pressurized process fluid that can cause injury from high-pressure release if a component fails, so personnel must wear appropriate PPE and stand to the side when operating manifold valves or working on impulse connections.','Impulse lines connecting pressure transmitters to the process can rupture due to corrosion, vibration fatigue, or freezing, releasing pressurized and potentially hazardous process fluid, so impulse lines must be regularly inspected, properly supported, and heat traced where freezing is possible, and any detected leaks must be reported and repaired immediately.','Working on pressure transmitter installations exposes personnel to process fluids that may be toxic, corrosive, flammable, or at elevated temperature, so the transmitter must be isolated using the manifold valves, the impulse lines must be drained, and appropriate PPE must be worn before opening any process-wetted connection.','Pressure transmitter electrical connections must be properly installed in rated enclosures appropriate for the area classification, and all wiring work must be performed by qualified personnel following proper procedures, as improper electrical connections in hazardous areas can create ignition sources, and incorrect wiring can cause false readings that lead to unsafe process conditions.','Exposure of a pressure transmitter sensing capsule to pressure significantly exceeding its rated range can rupture the sensing diaphragm, allowing process fluid to leak through the transmitter housing, so overpressure protection such as snubbers, siphons, or proper manifold valve procedures must be used, and the transmitter range must be selected to accommodate the maximum expected process pressure including transients.']
