        observations: ['Compare to lab results','Check sample system','Verify calibration','Monitor diagnostics','Watch trends'],
        safety: ['Process analyzers and their sample systems handle process fluids that may be toxic, corrosive, flammable, or at elevated temperature and pressure, creating chemical exposure risks at sample points, drain connections, and vent lines, so appropriate PPE must be worn, adequate ventilation must be maintained in analyzer shelters, and all sample system work must follow safe work procedures.','Calibration gases used for process analyzers may be toxic (such as hydrogen sulfide or carbon monoxide), flammable (such as hydrogen or methane), or asphyxiating (such as nitrogen), so calibration gas cylinders must be stored properly, regulators must be rated for the gas type, and calibration must be performed in well-ventilated areas with appropriate gas detection and PPE.','Process analyzers contain electrical components operating at various voltage levels, and some technologies such as flame ionization detectors use hydrogen fuel, so all electrical work must be performed by qualified personnel with proper lockout/tagout, intrinsic safety barriers must be maintained for hazardous area installations, and hydrogen supply lines must be regularly checked for leaks.','Analyzer sample systems operate under process pressure and may contain trapped pressure in sample lines, filters, and chambers even after the analyzer is isolated, so all pressure must be safely vented before opening any sample system component, and personnel must wear appropriate PPE including safety glasses and chemical-resistant gloves when working on sample systems.','Process analyzers may handle samples containing highly toxic materials such as hydrogen sulfide, hydrogen cyanide, benzene, or other hazardous substances, so personnel must review the safety data sheets for all possible sample stream components, ensure that analyzer shelter ventilation and gas detection are operational, and wear appropriate respiratory protection when working on sample systems.']
